WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.400
- So, to all of you,
"Barbie" has had many jobs,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.070
as are represented in our audience today.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.240
Lawyer, doctor, everything.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.883
So what were all of your first paid jobs?

00:00:09.717 --> 00:00:10.550
- (sighing) Oh, oh.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.640
- Look, I'm gonna start
with the sigh, okay?

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.627
- I would say I was a
little league umpire.

00:00:17.627 --> 00:00:20.040
- [Kelly] Oh.
(audience laughs)

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.000
- Shoulda checked the rules
before doing that gig,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.603
'cause, man, ooh, those parents

00:00:26.603 --> 00:00:29.880
on this side and on this side, what, what?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.190
And I'm like, I don't, "Was
it a ball, was it a strike?

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.643
What is the difference?"

00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:35.923
(all laughing)

00:00:35.923 --> 00:00:37.447
And, "Can she be running now?"

00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:39.393
"I don't know, she's out?

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.303
Is she out?

00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:43.030
I don't know, I'm sorry," oh God.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.936
- Oh my God, they just let anyone do it?

00:00:44.936 --> 00:00:45.780
- Right.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.980
- How did you get this job

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.813
if you didn't you know someone?

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:50.613
- No, I think I said I knew the rules,

00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:53.304
and I did on some level.

00:00:53.304 --> 00:00:57.330
They were there somewhere
and it didn't go so good.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.602
I got fired, I was fired
from my umpire job.

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:01.435
- [Kelly] Well, like, shocking.

00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:02.381
- Yep.

00:01:02.381 --> 00:01:03.411
(all laughing)

00:01:03.411 --> 00:01:05.820
- Yeah, they were like, "Find
someone that knows the rules."

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.440
You were an actor before
you were an actor.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.490
You know how actors are always,

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.439
- Yes, always lying on your resume.

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:11.660
- [Kelly] Lying on your resume,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.690
"I can totally ride a horse,
I'm totally an equestrian,

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:13.617
I'm totally this."

00:01:13.617 --> 00:01:14.940
You were just ahead of your game.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.460
America, what about you,
what was your first paid job?

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.830
- The first job I got paid to do,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.050
that wasn't like an official job,

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.800
was my next door neighbor
had a very big pot belly pig,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.808
and I got paid to clean up after the pig.

00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:29.970
That's what I did.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.760
- All right, making money
at a young age, I like it.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.260
- I was a hustler, you know?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.970
- I would take it where I could get it.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.310
- [Kelly] Absolutely, what about you Issa?

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:43.310
- Similar, I had a neighbor
who needed her cats babysat.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.333
That rhymes.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.270
And I did that at like,
eight, but then I discovered

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.329
that I was allergic so I
had to quit the next day.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:52.560
- [Kelly] Oh.
- Yeah.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.970
- [Kelly] I found out I was
allergic the hard way too.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.515
I rescued one and I was
like, "What is happening?"

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.494
And I just couldn't,
- Yeah, broke out in the face.

00:01:57.494 --> 00:01:58.327
- get it together, and we were like,

00:01:58.327 --> 00:01:59.210
"We are highly allergic to cats."

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.390
So I was like, super,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.550
- Devastated, I love cats.
- I found her a home.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.627
- Me too, I love cats.

00:02:03.627 --> 00:02:04.590
- But I can't mess with 'em.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.930
- No more than dogs 'cause, I'm right.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:07.800
But anyway,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.730
(all laughing)

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.240
I'm just kidding, but not.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.073
So what about you?

00:02:11.073 --> 00:02:12.679
What was, like, your first paid job?

00:02:12.679 --> 00:02:15.000
- I started, I was a hustler
too, I started young.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.920
I was working in a restaurant.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.600
I started when I was 10 working in, like,

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.530
the back in the kitchen.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.020
But my hustle started earlier than that.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.970
I used to actually, my
way of making money,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.330
before I was working at a restaurant,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.010
I would steal my brother's toys

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.380
and then sell them out on the street.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.213
(Kelly gasps)

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:34.207
- I know, And he'd get
so mad, he'd be like,

00:02:34.207 --> 00:02:35.123
"Where's my truck?"

00:02:35.123 --> 00:02:37.718
- And I was like, "I got two bucks worth."

00:02:37.718 --> 00:02:39.270
- Great!

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.560
- He was like, "Can I
have some of the money?"

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.770
I was like, "No, that wasn't
your store out on the street,

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.090
that was my store."

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.878
- So as a producer on this,

00:02:46.878 --> 00:02:48.420
what do you hope people take away?

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.420
Like, the messaging?

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.210
- I think, I mean, first
of all, it's so fun.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.730
I mean, when you see these
sets, it's like a dopamine hit.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.860
Like, you just are so happy

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.780
and the movie is so funny.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.410
It's also kind of unexpectedly
emotional, I have to say.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.930
And it's weird,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.790
I didn't think I would do a "Barbie" movie

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.260
and have so many conversations

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.170
about the meaning of life with,
you know, behind the scenes.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.490
You know, like, 'cause
when we're talking about,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.920
okay, "So life is perfect,
like if this is the utopia,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.000
what's the perfect ending?

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.850
What's the version of what is happiness?

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.683
What is life?"

00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:23.460
As all these questions came up
that we were trying to answer

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.726
so that we could kind of
round up this movie the way,

00:03:25.726 --> 00:03:27.300
you know, it needed to be.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.490
And it, you get, you end up having

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.430
these incredibly profound
conversations about life

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.370
and what's important, and what
makes really true happiness.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.860
And that is all woven into the movie.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.180
And there are quite, like,

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.207
you'll find moments where you're like,

00:03:40.207 --> 00:03:41.229
"Wow, that really hit me in,

00:03:41.229 --> 00:03:43.817
like a profound, emotional way."

00:03:43.817 --> 00:03:46.890
And overall, it's just, it's
hysterical and it's funny.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.050
It's a bunch of adults acting like,

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.030
having tantrums and being
children sometimes, so.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.040
And it's a beautifully designed world.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.090
It's just,
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.570
I think people are just
gonna really be happy

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.160
and have so much fun.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.530
We had a whole miniatures workshop.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.363
You could go, like,

00:04:02.363 --> 00:04:04.320
that hand paint tiny flamingos like this,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.730
that then I'd go on set,

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.010
and there'd be a flamingo
mailbox that life-size.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.230
I mean, everything was, you know,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.000
the painted backdrops,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.700
the dream houses that we
were actually, you know,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.890
going down the slide to the
pool and all that stuff.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.310
There was so much,
in-camera, kind of trickery,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.540
which is very satisfying

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.190
and it gives you that tangible feel.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.753
Like Greta said from the start she's like,

00:04:24.753 --> 00:04:26.610
- Yeah it feels like you
just jumped into the world.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.590
- you sorta feel like they can
reach in and grab everything

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.290
'cause that's would of play
with Barbies and stuff,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.333
so, it's really beautifully crafted.

